 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "in-plain-english",
   "heading": "In plain English",
   "role": "intuition",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "in-plain-english-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "Many choices have a budget: maximise satisfaction without spending more than your income. The Lagrange method turns such a problem into an unconstrained one by attaching a price to the constraint. At the best choice, the trade-off you face along the budget exactly matches the trade-off you value. The attached price, the multiplier, has its own meaning: how much better off you would be if the constraint were loosened by one unit."
      },
      {
       "t": "callout",
       "kind": "why",
       "title": "Why it matters",
       "md": "It is how almost every consumer, firm and planner problem in economics is solved, and the multiplier is a shadow price you can interpret.",
       "folded": false
      }
     ],
     "words": 99,
     "symbols": []
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "definition",
   "heading": "Definition",
   "role": "definition",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "definition-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "Maximize $f(\\mathbf{x})$ subject to $g(\\mathbf{x}) = c$:"
      },
      {
       "t": "math",
       "tex": "\\mathcal{L}(\\mathbf{x},\\lambda) = f(\\mathbf{x}) + \\lambda\\big(c - g(\\mathbf{x})\\big)"
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "First-order conditions:"
      },
      {
       "t": "math",
       "tex": "\\frac{\\partial\\mathcal{L}}{\\partial x_i} = f_i - \\lambda g_i = 0 \\;\\;\\forall i, \\qquad \\frac{\\partial\\mathcal{L}}{\\partial\\lambda} = c - g(\\mathbf{x}) = 0"
      }
     ],
     "words": 5,
     "symbols": [
      "$f(\\mathbf{x})$",
      "$g(\\mathbf{x}) = c$",
      "$\\mathcal{L}$",
      "$\\lambda$",
      "$f_i$, $g_i$",
      "$x$, $y$",
      "$\\mathbf{F}$, $\\mathbf{x}$, $\\mathbf{y}$",
      "$c_1$, $c_2$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "intuition",
   "heading": "Intuition",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "intuition-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "At the optimum the level set of $f$ is tangent to the constraint: $\\nabla f = \\lambda\\nabla g$. Dividing two FOCs gives the familiar tangency, e.g. $MU_x/MU_y = p_x/p_y$."
      }
     ],
     "words": 21,
     "symbols": [
      "$f(\\mathbf{x})$",
      "$g(\\mathbf{x}) = c$",
      "$\\lambda$",
      "$f_i$, $g_i$",
      "$p_x$, $p_y$, $m$",
      "$MU_x$, $MU_y$",
      "$U(x, y)$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "is-a-price",
   "heading": "λ is a price",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "is-a-price-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "$\\lambda^* = dV/dc$: how much the optimal value rises if the constraint is relaxed by one unit — the **shadow price**. In consumer theory it is the marginal utility of income. See [Envelope Theorem](note:envelope-theorem)."
      }
     ],
     "words": 30,
     "symbols": [
      "$V$",
      "$\\mathbf{x}^*$, $\\lambda^*$",
      "$c_1$, $c_2$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "worked-example",
   "heading": "Worked example",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "worked-example-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "Maximize $\\ln x + \\ln y$ subject to $2x + 5y = 100$."
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "FOCs: $1/x = 2\\lambda$ and $1/y = 5\\lambda$, so $2x = 5y$, each half of income. Solution: $x = 25$, $y = 10$, $\\lambda = 0.02$, $V = 5.521$. Raising income by one unit raises $V$ by about $0.02 = \\lambda$, which a numerical derivative confirms."
      }
     ],
     "words": 24,
     "symbols": [
      "$\\lambda$",
      "$V$",
      "$x$, $y$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "inequality-constraints-kuhn-tucker",
   "heading": "Inequality constraints (Kuhn–Tucker)",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "inequality-constraints-kuhn-tucker-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "For $g(\\mathbf{x}) \\leq c$: same Lagrangian, plus $\\lambda \\geq 0$ and **complementary slackness**, $\\lambda\\,(c - g(\\mathbf{x})) = 0$. Either the constraint binds, or its shadow price is zero."
      }
     ],
     "words": 17,
     "symbols": [
      "$g(\\mathbf{x}) = c$",
      "$\\lambda$",
      "$f_i$, $g_i$",
      "$\\mathbf{F}$, $\\mathbf{x}$, $\\mathbf{y}$",
      "$c_1$, $c_2$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "common-mistakes",
   "heading": "Common mistakes",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "common-mistakes-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "- Forgetting the constraint equation itself as an FOC.\n- Assuming the solution is interior when non-negativity binds (corner solutions).\n- Second-order conditions here use the *bordered* Hessian, not the plain Hessian of $f$."
      }
     ],
     "words": 33,
     "symbols": [
